  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nQuiet weather today and Saturday will turn more active on Sunday\nas the remnants of Hurricane Isaias lift north across the spine\nof the Appalachian Mtns and along the East Coast. This tropical\nsystem will quickly transition to extra-tropical and become\nbaroclinic as it moves north through AL/MS/GA. This system will\nbecome absorbed within the large scale synoptic flow later on\nSaturday and lift to the northeast Sat night into Sun morning. \n\nModel guidance/ensemble member consensus is now in very good\nagreement about bringing in the onset of rain from the south/sw\nto our forecast area around 5-7 AM Sunday morning...and\nspreading to the north/ne through the rest of the morning hours.\nPW values will reach into the 1-1.5 inch range in our area, with\nvalues greater than 1.5 staying along the coast and east of the\nI-95 corridor. This system will become fairly fast-moving as\nwell, which will limit the threat of heavy rain on Sunday. \n\nModel soundings are indicating a layer of potentially unstable\nair above a very strong inversion around 4-5 kft. This mid level\ninstability is relatively shallow and while we cannot completely\nrule out the threat for a few thunderstorms and resulting locally\nheavier downpours, confidence is on the low side for this to\noccur. So, the latest thinking is for a widespread 0.5 to 1\ninch of rain from Sunday morning to Monday morning. This amount\nof rain over this time period should be manageable and not cause\nany impacts in our area. \n\nWinds should remain sustained around 20 mph or less with some\nridgetop gusts up to 30 mph possible in the Poconos and\nCatskills. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThere may be some lingering lake enhanced rain showers streaming\nin from the north/nw Monday morning, but the overall trend\nthrough the rest of the day should be to dry conditions out.\nWeak high pressure builds in on Monday night before more rain \narrives later in the week. \n\nThere is still quite a bit of uncertainty with respect to the\ntiming of the next short wave and associated rain. Some of the\nearliest guidance has rain moving in late Monday night and Tue\nmorning, while later solutions have rain entering the region\nfrom the west/nw on Tuesday evening. Rainfall amounts will\nlikely range from a tenth of an inch, up to a half inch before\nending Wed morning. Another ridge builds in Wed and Thu prior to\nanother round of rain lat Thu into Friday followed by what looks\nto be some of the coldest air of the season so far. We'll have\nto see how the guidance trends with this incoming deep/cold\ntrough next weekend, but it is something to keep an eye on\nthrough next week.",
